+// GetQueuedCompletionStatus has three outcomes, not two, and both loops below depend
+// on telling them apart. It returns TRUE when it dequeues a packet for an operation
+// that succeeded. It returns FALSE with a completion when it dequeues a packet for an
+// operation that FAILED - the read is over either way, and the entry it left on
+// m_listPendingIO is this thread's to remove, because no one else takes anything off
+// this port. It returns FALSE without a completion when it dequeued nothing at all.
+// Reading the return value alone folds the middle case into the last one, and then
+// whichever loop asked the question ends on it: the drain below loses that read for
+// good, and the wait above it ends the thread that feeds every upload. Answer the
+// question the loops actually have - "is there a completion to deal with" - and let
+// the failure travel with it as a zero byte count, which is what a failed read moved.
+bool CUploadDiskIOThread::TakeCompletion(DWORD dwTimeout, DWORD &rdwRead, ULONG_PTR &rKey, OverlappedRead_Struct *&rpIO)
+{
+	rdwRead = 0;
+	rKey = 0;
+	rpIO = NULL;
+	if (::GetQueuedCompletionStatus(m_hPort, &rdwRead, &rKey, (LPOVERLAPPED*)&rpIO, dwTimeout))
+		return true;
+	rdwRead = 0;
+	return rpIO != NULL;
+}
